No code or structure of evcc's charger/em2go*.go or charger/daheimladen.go is
used (those files are not MIT licensed). Behaviour learned from evcc's MIT
core (loadpoint phase and current logic) is re-implemented in our own words.

=============================================================================
evcc core - custom_components/gnlt_charger/site/ (and tests/test_site_*.py,
tests/site_fakes.py), modbus/commands.py (Commander._run_phase_direct) and
the reconnect behaviour of modbus/client.py

Portions are ported from evcc (https://github.com/evcc-io/evcc), master of
20.11.2026, commit 82d37e922b2c0278d56eb90c5fdd3c0cd2c62175: core/loadpoint*.go,
core/site*.go, core/circuit/circuit.go, core/prioritizer/prioritizer.go,
util/grid/grid.go, util/modbus, api/ (types and interfaces). The phase-switch
rule of Loadpoint.scalePhases is used in modbus/commands.py and
modbus/sequences.py: while the charger is enabled (charging, an armed start, an
open charge or Plug & Charge) a switch to 3 phases from a station not on 3
phases first drops reg 91 to the minimum current, and 200 = 3 is written only
if that write went out (scalePhases returns when setLimit fails); there is
no drop before 3 -> 1; the whole-amp firmware class restarts at the minimum
current when it goes to 3 phases. The user's current is put back afterwards. Each ported file carries a header naming evcc as the
source. evcc core is published under the MIT License below.

